Modifications
=============

sad-web-push 0.12.0
-------------------
Building on sad-web-push 0.11.1, the 0.12.0 release line includes:

- Ported Declarative Web Push `Notification` and `NotificationAction` builders
  (W3C Push API draft format serialization) from upstream PR
  pimeys/rust-web-push#72 by Daniel Dickison (with `Clone`/`PartialEq`/`Eq`
  derives and rustdoc/doctest additions).
- Upgraded HTTP types to `http 1.x` across `request_builder` and all built-in
  transports.
- Replaced the default transport with `HyperWebPushClient` built on `hyper 1.x`,
  `hyper-util`, `http-body-util`, and `hyper-rustls 0.27` using an explicit
  `ring` `CryptoProvider` and OS native root loading (`rustls-native-certs`),
  with no OpenSSL or libcurl library dependency in the default 0.12 feature
  configuration.
- Added optional `HyperNativeTlsWebPushClient` (`hyper-native-tls` feature,
  `hyper-tls 0.6`) and upgraded optional `IsahcWebPushClient` (`isahc-client`
  feature) to `isahc 2.x` with send-time URL validation and per-request
  `RedirectPolicy::None` enforcement.
- Added `SafeHttpConnector` (used by `HyperWebPushClient` and
  `HyperNativeTlsWebPushClient`) to validate endpoint URIs at send time, check
  every DNS-resolved address against the configured IP policy on each new
  connection, and dial checked socket addresses directly (preserving URI-host
  TLS verification and DNS-name SNI) to mitigate DNS rebinding between
  validation and connection, with opt-in `allow_insecure_endpoints(true)` on
  client builders for local testing.
- Added `WebPushMessage::vapid_signature` directly on `WebPushMessage` so
  empty-payload pushes keep `payload: None` while emitting a single sensitive
  `Authorization: vapid ...` header, and made `vapid_signature` authoritative
  in `try_build_request`.
- Added `WebPushError::TooManyRequests` (HTTP 429 with `Retry-After`) and
  `WebPushError::Timeout` (covering Hyper connect/request deadlines and Isahc
  request/body-read timeouts).
- Raised the RFC 8291 `aes128gcm` plaintext payload limit to 3,993 bytes
  (producing a 4,096-byte encoded HTTP body including the 86-byte header,
  1-byte delimiter, and 16-byte GCM tag; `rs = 4,096`, encrypted record + tag
  = 4,010 bytes), keeping legacy `aesgcm` at 3,800 plaintext bytes.

sad-web-push 0.11.1
-------------------
Changes relative to web-push 0.11.0 (commit 8de73e2e):

- Cherry-picked unreleased upstream documentation improvements for
  WebPushMessageBuilder (commit d4d339b4 by Niklas Fiekas, rust-web-push#77).
- Replaced Mozilla's MPL-2.0 / OpenSSL-backed `ece` crate, `jwt-simple`
  (which pulled in `crypto-bigint 0.5.5`, referenced by RUSTSEC-2026-0387),
  `sec1_decode`, `pem`, and `chrono` (referenced by informational
  RUSTSEC-2026-0543) with pure-Rust implementations built on `p256 0.14`,
  `aes-gcm 0.11`, `hkdf 0.13`, `sha2 0.11`, `pem-rfc7468 1.0`, `getrandom 0.4`,
  `zeroize 1.8`, and `httpdate 1.0`. Enabled Serde's `derive` feature in place
  of a direct `serde_derive` dependency.
- Implemented RFC 8188 / RFC 8291 (`aes128gcm`) and legacy
  draft-ietf-webpush-encryption-04 (`aesgcm`) payload encryption in pure Rust,
  clearing selected library-owned secret buffers (ephemeral keys, ECDH secrets,
  IKM, CEK, nonce, and plaintext records) on drop.
- Implemented pure-Rust ES256 VAPID JWT signing and bounded (65,536-byte) SEC1
  / PKCS#8 PEM, DER, and fixed-size raw base64url key parsing, fixing the panic
  on wrong-length base64 private keys (rust-web-push#73,
  https://github.com/pimeys/rust-web-push/issues/73, reported by a-kolechko).
- Hardened builder endpoint validation (default canonical HTTPS with nonempty
  host, no embedded userinfo, valid non-zero explicit ports, and rejection of
  localhost names, non-canonical numeric IPv4 forms, and disallowed special-use
  IPv4/IPv6 ranges including restricting IPv6 literals to 2000::/3 global
  unicast minus selected special-purpose and transition ranges, with opt-in
  `allow_insecure_endpoint(true)` for local testing), added fallible
  `request_builder::try_build_request` and `request_builder::parse_retry_after`
  (capped at 31,536,000 seconds), added `Debug` redaction for secrets and
  endpoint paths, marked `Authorization` headers sensitive, removed endpoint
  paths and headers from built-in client logs, enforced a 65,536-byte response
  body cap (Hyper checks before appending each chunk; Isahc reads up to 65,537
  bytes to detect overflow), added default HTTP timeouts (30s request / 10s
  connect) and `with_timeout` constructors, enforced per-request
  `RedirectPolicy::None` on `IsahcWebPushClient`, preserved VAPID headers on
  empty-payload pushes, attached `Retry-After` on HTTP 429 responses,
  reconciled the 3,800-byte payload size limit, re-exported `ErrorInfo` at the
  crate root, and declared MSRV Rust 1.89.0.
- The crate is published as `sad-web-push` (library `sad_web_push`).

The upstream changes listed above were written by their upstream authors; see
CHANGELOG.md for attribution of each change. The packaging changes, pure-Rust
cryptographic implementation, transport modernization, and Sadco Security Team
fixes listed above were developed by the Sadco Security Team with assistance
from AI coding tools (Anthropic Claude, Google Gemini).
